Firmware for the Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-7 carrying the
**ESP32-S3-WROOM-1-N16R8** module — 16 MB of flash, 8 MB of PSRAM. The partition
table does not fit an 8 MB module, and vendor documentation for this board
describes one that at least some units are not, so trust the marking printed on
the module.

{{CHANGES}}

## Install

On a panel that has never run Slate, from a Chromium-based desktop browser with
the board on a USB cable: **[{{PAGE}}]({{PAGE}})**. No toolchain, no ESP-IDF, no
command line. What happens after the flash is on the screen, and the walkthrough
is in [the README](https://github.com/{{REPO}}#first-run).

The same four images with `esptool` instead:

```
esptool.py --chip esp32s3 write_flash \
  0x0     bootloader-{{VERSION}}.bin \
  0x8000  partition-table-{{VERSION}}.bin \
  0x1d000 ota-data-initial-{{VERSION}}.bin \
  0x20000 slate-{{VERSION}}.bin
```

A panel that is **already running Slate** needs neither of those, and normally
needs nothing at all: it checks once a day against the manifest published beside
the installer page above, and what it finds waits in the editor's **Firmware
updates** panel until somebody presses *Install*. Nothing is downloaded by the
check, and a wall panel does not get to reboot itself in the middle of an
evening. It verifies the SHA-256 the manifest names before it makes the image
bootable, and the bootloader returns to the previous version on its own if the
new one does not come back.

To take this release now rather than at the next daily check, the same install
over the API — nothing downloaded from this page, nothing cloned:

```
# answers {"token":"…"}; drop the -d if the panel has no administrator PIN
curl -sS -X POST http://<address>/api/v1/session \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"pin":"<PIN>"}'
export SLATE_TOKEN=<the token from that answer>

# look at the channel now, without waiting for the daily check
curl -sS -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $SLATE_TOKEN" \
     http://<address>/api/v1/update/check

# until "state" is "idle" and "available" names {{VERSION}}
curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $SLATE_TOKEN" \
     http://<address>/api/v1/update

curl -sS -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $SLATE_TOKEN" \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"version":"{{VERSION}}"}' \
     http://<address>/api/v1/update/install
```

The middle request is not optional. `check` answers when it has accepted the
request, not when it has an answer, and `install` takes the offer the panel is
holding by then: `409 busy` while the check is still running, `409 no_update` if
it is holding no offer at all. Naming the version is what keeps this from
installing something else that arrived in between — a mismatch is refused rather
than silently obeyed.

`slate-{{VERSION}}.bin` on this page is for what that channel does not cover — a
panel with no route to the internet, or a return to this version after a later
one has replaced it on the installer page. It goes to the development upload
endpoint, which has no manifest, no signature and no HTTPS behind it and is not
meant to: it is a LAN mechanism between a desk and a wall, behind that same
token.

```
curl -sS -X POST http://<address>/api/v1/ota/upload \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer $SLATE_TOKEN" \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/octet-stream' -H 'Expect:' \
     --data-binary @slate-{{VERSION}}.bin
```

The `Expect:` is not decoration: without it curl waits out a `100-continue` the
panel never answers. From a clone of the repository, `tools/ota/upload.sh
<address> slate-{{VERSION}}.bin` is that request with the checks worth having
around it — it refuses an image built for another chip before a megabyte goes
over WiFi, and waits for the panel to come back and report the version it was
sent.

Every path here keeps the dashboard, authentication settings, External API keys
and WiFi credentials, which live outside the application partitions. A full
flash keeps them too unless the flash is erased first — the browser installer
offers that as a checkbox and says what it costs.

## Verify

```
{{CHECKSUMS}}
```

`slate-{{VERSION}}.elf` and `slate-{{VERSION}}.map` are what a core dump from
this image symbolicates against, and `sdkconfig-{{VERSION}}.txt` is the exact
configuration it was built from. Neither is needed to run the firmware; both are
needed to read a crash report about it.

## Third-party software

`slate-{{VERSION}}.bin` is not all Slate's code: LVGL draws it, littlefs stores
its configuration, ESP-IDF and the toolchain run it, the editor inside it is
built with React, three font faces are compiled in, and the certificate
authorities it trusts are Mozilla's. Nor is any of those all its own author's
code — LVGL, ESP-IDF and the toolchain each vendor libraries they did not write,
and those travel in the image too. Each arrives under its own license, and
[`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`](https://github.com/{{REPO}}/blob/v{{VERSION}}/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md)
names them all, with the complete texts in
[`LICENSES/`](https://github.com/{{REPO}}/tree/v{{VERSION}}/LICENSES). Links
rather than files, but links to the tag this image was built from: somebody who
downloaded a binary has no clone to read those notices out of.

Slate itself is MIT-licensed.
